---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro'
import RecipeCard from '../../components/RecipeCard.astro'

const macro = `defaults:
  who: sam@example.com
steps:
  - fill: { label: Email }
    value: $who
  - click: { role: button, name: Sign in }`

const use = `setup:
  - use: sign-in
  - use: sign-in
    with: { who: admin@example.com }`

const data = `setup:
  - each: $orders
    as: order
    steps:
      - click: { text: '\${order.name}' }`

const literal = `setup:
  - each: [Acme Corp, Globex, Initech]
    as: customer
    steps:
      - fill: { label: Search }
        value: $customer
      - click: { role: button, name: Search }`
---

<Docs
  title="Macros and data"
  description="Sharing setup between recipes, and driving one from a list."
>
  <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold">Macros and data</h1>

  <p class="mt-6 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    Setup shared between recipes goes in <code>paths.macros</code>, one file per macro. The filename
    is its name.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={macro} file="screenshots/macros/sign-in.yaml" />
  </div>
  <div class="mt-4">
    <RecipeCard code={use} file="screenshots/recipes/dashboard.yaml" />
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">Data files</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    Files in <code>paths.data</code> are in scope by filename:
    <code>screenshots/data/orders.yaml</code> is
    <code>$orders</code>, and
    <code>each: $orders</code> iterates it.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={data} file="screenshots/recipes/orders.yaml" />
  </div>

  <p class="mt-6 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    <code>each</code> also takes the list itself, for the handful of values not worth a file.
  </p>
  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={literal} file="screenshots/recipes/searches.yaml" />
  </div>

  <p class="mt-6 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    The filename becomes a <code>$name</code>, so keep it to letters, digits and underscores — a <code
      >$</code
    > reference stops at a hyphen, and <code>open-orders.yaml</code> cannot be reached. Group what would
    have been several hyphenated files into one and read them by path:
    <code>$orders.open</code>,
    <code>$orders.shipped</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="writing-a-reference" class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">Writing a reference</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    <code>$name</code> and
    <code>{'${name}'}</code> are the same thing, and
    <code>$a.b</code> reads a path into whatever
    <code>$a</code> holds. The braces matter in one place: where the reference runs into text that would
    otherwise be read as part of the name. A name runs on through letters, digits, underscores and dots,
    so
    <code>Order $order.name.</code> looks for a path ending in a dot and <code
      >{'Order ${order.name}.'}</code
    > does not.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    <span class="font-medium text-ink"
      >A reference that is the whole value keeps that value's type.</span
    >
    <code>each: $orders</code> gets the list, not a rendering of one. A reference sitting inside a longer
    string becomes text, because that is all a string can hold.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    Three things fill the same table: data files, by filename;
    <code>with:</code> on a
    <code>use:</code>, for the macro it calls; and
    <code>readValue: … as: total</code>, which puts what an input holds into <code>$total</code> for the
    steps after it.
    <code>each: … as:</code> binds its name for its own steps only.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    <span class="font-medium text-ink">References are substituted in steps, and nowhere else.</span>
    <code>clip</code>,
    <code>marks</code> and
    <code>callouts</code> are read as written, so
    <code>text: $total</code> on a callout draws those six characters. Steps are where a recipe varies;
    what it points at afterwards is fixed.
  </p>

  <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    <span class="font-medium text-ink">
      <code class="font-mono">{'${env.NAME}'}</code> is the operator's, not the config's.</span
    > It resolves only for the names a run was given with
    <code>--allow-env</code> or <code>SHOTLIST_ENV</code>, which is how a <a
      href="/docs/running-shotlist#signing-in">sign-in macro</a
    > gets a password without one being written down. A name that was not allowed does not resolve however
    it is set, and an
    <code>--untrusted</code> run resolves none of them.
  </p>

  <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-(--muted)">
    A reference reads what the data holds and nothing else. Keys that belong to the language —
    <code>__proto__</code>,
    <code>constructor</code>,
    <code>prototype</code> — resolve to nothing, and a reference that matches nothing is an error rather
    than an empty string. A literal
    <code>$</code> inside a longer string is left alone, so a shot of "costs $5" survives.
  </p>
</Docs>
